 ## Background
-The foundation of this family lies in the S3 Texture Compression (S3TC) and its successors, developed by S3 Graphics in the late 1990s and early 2000s for real-time 3D graphics. BC7 (Block Compression 7) was introduced by ARM and AMD as part of the OpenGL Extension GL_EXT_texture_compression_bc7, offering high-quality compression for complex color data [S1, unofficial]. ASTC (Adaptive Scalable Texture Compression) was developed by ARM as a more flexible alternative, supporting variable block sizes and bit rates [S1, S2]. In the context of volume rendering and neural rendering, these formats have been adapted to compress texture atlases that store splat attributes (positions, colors, opacities) or baked neural field representations, leveraging the existing GPU hardware pipeline to avoid the memory bottleneck of storing dense FP16/FP32 volumes [S1, unofficial].
